### Why This Matters

This anomalous expansion is crucial for life on Earth. Because ice floats, it forms an insulating layer on top of lakes and oceans in winter, preventing them from freezing solid from bottom to top and protecting aquatic ecosystems. If water behaved like most other substances and contracted when freezing, ice would sink, bodies of water would freeze from the bottom up, and much of aquatic life would not survive cold winters.

The maximum density of water actually occurs at approximately **4°C** (39°F). As water cools from room temperature down to 4°C, it contracts normally. Below 4°C, the hydrogen bond network begins reorganizing into the hexagonal structure, and the density decreases until it reaches its solid form. [3](#c97756d8-610d-4d68-a741-7fa18acceca7)

