## What SOLID Stands For in Software Design

**SOLID** is an acronym for five foundational design principles in object-oriented software engineering. These principles were introduced by Robert C. Martin and later named **SOLID** by Michael Feathers to help developers write code that is easier to maintain, scale, and reason about. [1](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d) [2](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d)

The five letters stand for:

- **S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP):** A class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should take on just one responsibility and handle it well. [1](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d) [2](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d)
- **O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP):** Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification, allowing new behavior to be added without altering existing code. [1](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d) [3](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d)
- **L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):** Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without breaking the correctness of the program. [1](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d) [2](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d)
- **I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):** Clients should not be forced to depend on interfaces they do not use; prefer smaller, specific interfaces over large, general-purpose ones. [1](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d) [2](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d)
- **D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP):** High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; instead, both should depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations. [1](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d) [2](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d)

By applying these principles, developers reduce coupling between classes, which makes codebases easier to test, extend, and refactor as projects grow. [2](#0395b8c6-5bc2-4158-9408-0ce602366d0d)

