### Why Caddy Wins on Simplicity

- **Zero-configuration TLS:** Once you provide a hostname, Caddy automatically obtains, installs, and renews certificates from Let's Encrypt or ZeroSSL. HTTPS is enabled by default with no opt-in step, and in many cases you write no ACME-specific configuration at all. [1](#2b5610ff-4a89-4b0b-a420-8edb60ad60f3) [2](#9895af82-205c-46f0-a731-6013651f71cc)
- **No external tooling required:** Unlike nginx, Caddy does not need a separate ACME client such as Certbot or cert-manager. The entire certificate lifecycle is handled inside the single server binary, eliminating the overhead of managing sidecars or cron jobs. [1](#2b5610ff-4a89-4b0b-a420-8edb60ad60f3)
- **Security-first defaults:** Caddy handles OCSP stapling and can fall back to a secondary certificate authority automatically if the primary is down. Modern features like HTTP/3 over QUIC are also enabled automatically once TLS is active. [1](#2b5610ff-4a89-4b0b-a420-8edb60ad60f3)

### How Traefik Compares

Traefik supports automatic HTTPS natively, but it is more involved. It demands explicit configuration of certificate resolvers, challenge types, and storage paths before it will issue or renew certificates. [1](#2b5610ff-4a89-4b0b-a420-8edb60ad60f3) While its Docker label-based service discovery is powerful, the initial TLS setup requires understanding entrypoints, routers, and middleware chains. [1](#2b5610ff-4a89-4b0b-a420-8edb60ad60f3)

### How Nginx Compares

Nginx is the most manual option. It has no native certificate provisioning and relies on external tools like Certbot, requiring manual configuration of certificate paths and renewal schedules. [1](#2b5610ff-4a89-4b0b-a420-8edb60ad60f3) This creates multiple moving parts instead of one and leaves more room for human error or expired certificates. [1](#2b5610ff-4a89-4b0b-a420-8edb60ad60f3)

### Bottom Line

If your priority is the least painful path to automatic HTTPS, **Caddy** is the clear choice: it is the only one of the three that makes TLS a zero-configuration default. Traefik provides automation but requires upfront setup, while nginx remains a largely manual process dependent on external tools. If you already run Caddy in production, this consensus aligns with the day-to-day experience of not maintaining Certbot or certificate resolvers.

